            <ArticleTitle>Posterior epidural migration of a sequestrated lumbar disk fragment: MR imaging findings.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1592-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present a 75-year-old man who, for 2 weeks, had progressive pain in both of his thighs when standing straight. MR imaging showed a sequestrated disk fragment, which had a signal intensity similar to that of a herniated disk with a rim enhancement in the posterior epidural space and a ruptured outermost annulus of the intervertebral disk at L2-3. Awareness of these MR imaging findings can help in the diagnosis of posterior epidural disk migration.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
